  "salt": "_2Ipyg7Y3-HwrEwYZabACw==",
  "text": "The Fir Bolg are a legendary race of Ireland. The Book of Invasions, a mythical history, tells how Ireland was settled in several waves. Different peoples arrived over time, and the Fir Bolg grew legendary among them. Older scholars tied their name to the word for a bag and called them the men of the bags. Modern study links the name to the Builg or Belgae, the Celtic tribes that gave Belgium its name. The people may trace descent from a god named Bolg or Bulg. Their name would then mean the sons of the god Bolg. The Fir Bolg came from the children of Nemed. After the Fomorians drove them out, Nemed's son Starn reached Greece. His descendants stayed in slavery there for 230 years. They carried bags of earth to raise hills. Later they used the bags to build boats and sail away. They returned to Ireland under Dela, who came at the feast of Lughnasa. Dela split the island among his sons. Gann and Sengann took Munster, Slane took Leinster, and Rudraige gained Ulster. Eochaid mac Eirc ruled the middle province with his wife Tailtiu. Rinnal invented spear points, and Delga built the fort of Dun Delgan, now Dundalk. The Fir Bolg ruled for 37 years before the Tuatha De Danann arrived. At the first battle of Mag Tuired, king Nuada drove the Fir Bolg out of Ireland. He lost an arm and his throne in the fight. Some Fir Bolg may have retreated to the Aran Islands, where Aonghus raised Dun Aongusa. Others may have gone to the Scottish islands and to Connemara. Place-names such as Dun Bolg and Moyboulogue recall this race.",
